; 04: a grid of bytes costs 8 bytes a cell (0026's packed bytes).
;
; Every array element is an i64 (spec 10.1), so a maze of 2001 by 2001
; cells, each a wall or not, is 32 MB where 4 MB would hold it. This
; program holds that grid and nothing else:
;
;   ./04 4004001     peak 32.0 MB (measured), for 4 MB of information
;
; What it costs in the programs of this round, measured:
;   maze 2001 by 2001  peak 127 MB: cells 32, distances 32, queue 32, and
;                      the reader's last doubling; packed cells would save
;                      28 MB of 127. Python, with bytes for the cells and a
;                      list for the distances, peaks at 118 MB.
;   life 1000 by 1000  peak 25.5 MB for two boards of 8 MB; packed, 2 MB.
;                      Python's list-of-lists version peaks at 46.5 MB.
;
; So grids do not earn 0026: every program here fits comfortably and is at
; or under Python's memory. The distances and the queue need full ints
; anyway. A grid of 20,000 by 20,000 would: 3.2 GB of cells as words.

(def main () int
  (let n (int_prefix (arg 0) 0)
    (let g (array_set (array_new n 0) (sub n 1) 1)
      (write_line (digits (add (array_len g) (array_get g (sub n 1))))))))
